  "query_preview": "..\nTonight: Winds and convection will start to ease stating late \nevening and overnight. Winds will remain breezy through the \nnight with gusts still peaking into the low 30 mph range across \nthe Basin. The colder air mass will lead to a drop in overnight \ntemperatures. Lows will be in the 30s to low 40s. \n\nSaturday through Thursday: Dry air mass keeps the region precip\nfree through Monday. High temperatures will be near normal to \nslightly below normal in the 60s. Overnight lows continue to \ntrend cooler, increasing the chance for many locations in the \nnorthern valleys and parts of the Palouse and Spokane area \npossibly reaching their first frost and/or freeze. Many \nlocations in the northern valleys and even parts of the Spokane \narea to Palouse will see their first frost or freeze with this \npattern. Clouds increase Monday evening with the next system. \nThat system will bring beneficial rains and high mountain snow \nprimarily to the Cascades and north Idaho thanks to strong zonal\nflow. There is a 40-60% chance of 0.5 inches of rain near the \nCascade crest and a 5-10% chance for the Idaho Panhandle from \nTuesday morning to Wednesday morning. Conditions look cool, but \ndry after this system with minor shortwave ridging mid to late \nweek.",
